WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.006
Continuo, part four.

2
00:00:01.273 --> 00:00:02.294
The band on the laptop.

3
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:07.033
Every note arrives at the laptop as a few

4
00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:09.396
small messages: when it started, which

5
00:00:09.396 --> 00:00:11.297
string, how hard, and which note.

6
00:00:11.743 --> 00:00:14.077
The laptop is where the band lives: a

7
00:00:14.077 --> 00:00:16.357
drummer and a bassist, two small models

8
00:00:16.357 --> 00:00:17.660
that learned from songs.

9
00:00:17.985 --> 00:00:20.157
Each of them decides, every sixteenth

10
00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:22.219
note, before that sixteenth arrives.

11
00:00:22.545 --> 00:00:23.848
This video is about how.

12
00:00:27.503 --> 00:00:29.783
Before the band comes in, Sena counts in:

13
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:30.574
four even strums.

14
00:00:30.988 --> 00:00:33.390
The grid takes its tempo from those four

15
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.319
strums: one bar, cut into sixteen slots,

16
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:38.310
at whatever tempo Sena is playing.

17
00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:40.537
Each slot is one sixteenth note.

18
00:00:41.547 --> 00:00:42.960
After that it keeps listening.

19
00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:45.361
If he pushes ahead or pulls back a little,

20
00:00:45.361 --> 00:00:46.491
the grid moves with him.

21
00:00:46.773 --> 00:00:48.515
It follows the player, not a click.

22
00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:50.518
Now the notes.

23
00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:53.008
Each one lands wherever Sena played it,

24
00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:54.564
and snaps to its nearest slot.

25
00:00:54.876 --> 00:00:57.107
So a slot either has a note in it, or it

26
00:00:57.107 --> 00:00:57.573
does not.

27
00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:00.219
From here on the band thinks in slots, not

28
00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:00.790
in seconds.

29
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.703
Here is the guitar, part way through a

30
00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:06.904
bar.

31
00:01:07.206 --> 00:01:09.420
Below it, three of the nine drums, with

32
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.231
what the drummer has already played.

33
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:12.388
This line is now.

34
00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.889
From each guitar slot the drummer reads

35
00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:17.270
only a few things: was there a note, how

36
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.502
busy the guitar has been lately, and where

37
00:01:19.502 --> 00:01:20.395
we are in the bar.

38
00:01:20.692 --> 00:01:21.436
Not which note.

39
00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:24.065
The drummer never hears pitch; for rhythm

40
00:01:24.065 --> 00:01:25.057
it does not need it.

41
00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:28.676
The drummer cannot wait to hear what comes

42
00:01:28.676 --> 00:01:28.927
next.

43
00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:31.392
It has to decide the next slot before that

44
00:01:31.392 --> 00:01:33.454
slot arrives, so it does not know what

45
00:01:33.454 --> 00:01:34.511
Sena will play there.

46
00:01:35.459 --> 00:01:37.126
So at every slot it looks back over

47
00:01:37.126 --> 00:01:38.978
everything the guitar has played so far.

48
00:01:39.256 --> 00:01:41.432
The most recent slots count the most; the

49
00:01:41.432 --> 00:01:42.173
older ones fade.

50
00:01:42.451 --> 00:01:44.442
It can see back about four minutes, at a

51
00:01:44.442 --> 00:01:46.109
hundred and twenty beats per minute.

52
00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:48.517
That is how it keeps a groove going: the

53
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:50.323
pattern it has heard over the last few

54
00:01:50.323 --> 00:01:51.481
bars shapes the next hit.

55
00:01:51.948 --> 00:01:53.890
And it never listens to its own drums.

56
00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:55.781
Nothing it played goes back in.

57
00:01:56.088 --> 00:01:58.081
It is not guessing Sena's next note,

58
00:01:58.081 --> 00:01:58.439
either.

59
00:01:58.746 --> 00:01:59.921
It is choosing its own.

60
00:02:01.466 --> 00:02:04.073
Out come nine numbers, one for each drum:

61
00:02:04.073 --> 00:02:06.149
how likely that drum should hit in the

62
00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:06.681
next slot.

63
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.437
Each drum has its own line.

64
00:02:08.943 --> 00:02:10.238
Over the line, it hits.

65
00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:12.674
Here the kick and the hi-hat cross, so

66
00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:14.074
both land in the next slot.

67
00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:16.665
Several drums can hit at once, or none at

68
00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:16.872
all.

69
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:18.842
The snare stays quiet this time.

70
00:02:19.853 --> 00:02:22.031
The drummer is a small transformer, two

71
00:02:22.031 --> 00:02:24.209
layers and four attention heads, and it

72
00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:26.076
learned all this from more than ten

73
00:02:26.076 --> 00:02:27.943
thousand songs with real drum parts.

74
00:02:28.254 --> 00:02:29.551
Nobody wrote it a groove.

75
00:02:29.862 --> 00:02:31.936
One small hand-written rule adds a kick

76
00:02:31.936 --> 00:02:34.373
and a crash at the start of a phrase; the

77
00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:36.292
rest it picked up from real drummers.

78
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:42.034
The bassist is the same kind of model,

79
00:02:42.034 --> 00:02:43.221
with more to listen to.

80
00:02:43.531 --> 00:02:45.855
It hears the guitar's past, the notes Sena

81
00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:48.179
last played, still ringing on as far as it

82
00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:50.244
knows, and the drums the drummer just

83
00:02:50.244 --> 00:02:51.277
chose for this slot.

84
00:02:51.587 --> 00:02:53.652
That last one is how the bass sits with

85
00:02:53.652 --> 00:02:54.117
the kick.

86
00:02:55.055 --> 00:02:57.031
First it decides: play, or rest.

87
00:02:57.335 --> 00:02:58.956
One number against its own line.

88
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.527
Over the line: play.

89
00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:01.945
Rest is a real choice.

90
00:03:02.249 --> 00:03:04.428
A bassist that played every slot would not

91
00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:05.491
sound like a bassist.

92
00:03:05.907 --> 00:03:07.246
If it plays, which note?

93
00:03:07.555 --> 00:03:09.873
Twelve choices, counted up from the lowest

94
00:03:09.873 --> 00:03:11.007
note Sena last played.

95
00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:12.398
The tallest bar wins.

96
00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:13.617
How high or low?

97
00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:16.262
That is a simple rule: the octave nearest

98
00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.709
the last bass note it played.

99
00:03:18.202 --> 00:03:20.195
Because everything is counted up from

100
00:03:20.195 --> 00:03:22.397
Sena's lowest note, it works in any key.

101
00:03:22.712 --> 00:03:25.019
Change key, and the bassist does not need

102
00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:25.439
to know.

103
00:03:25.753 --> 00:03:27.798
It learned its choices from songs with

104
00:03:27.798 --> 00:03:28.637
real bass lines.

105
00:03:32.493 --> 00:03:34.781
Both choices go to a synthesiser, and out

106
00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:36.912
of the speakers, placed exactly on the

107
00:03:36.912 --> 00:03:37.796
slot's grid line.

108
00:03:38.108 --> 00:03:40.448
If a hit were ever going to be late, it is

109
00:03:40.448 --> 00:03:42.111
dropped rather than played late.

110
00:03:42.984 --> 00:03:45.251
Then now moves on one sixteenth, and the

111
00:03:45.251 --> 00:03:47.519
whole thing happens again: slot after

112
00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:49.786
slot, the band plays along, always one

113
00:03:49.786 --> 00:03:50.366
slot ahead.

114
00:03:50.683 --> 00:03:52.317
Every slot is a fresh decision.

115
00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:54.848
Nothing is planned further ahead than one

116
00:03:54.848 --> 00:03:57.327
sixteenth, so when Sena's playing changes,

117
00:03:57.327 --> 00:03:58.698
the band changes with him.

118
00:04:01.276 --> 00:04:03.794
This is the laptop's live page in the lab,

119
00:04:03.794 --> 00:04:06.312
on the seventeenth of October, here with a

120
00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.774
simple rule bass: a note on every kick.

121
00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.180
Guitar above, drums and bass below.

122
00:04:19.901 --> 00:04:21.826
Drums and bass, one slot ahead of the

123
00:04:21.826 --> 00:04:23.463
guitar, for as long as he plays.

124
00:04:24.469 --> 00:04:26.234
But how fast does all this have to be?

125
00:04:26.513 --> 00:04:27.581
That is the next video.

